<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qutb Minar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Qutb Minar complex</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Qutb Minar is a tall minaret of red sandstone in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>, begun around the end of the twelfth century under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qutb-ud-din-aibak">Qutb ud-Din Aibak</a> and completed by his successors. It stands within a complex that includes one of the earliest mosques in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It exemplifies the Indo-Islamic architectural synthesis of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/qutb-minar.jpg" alt="Qutb Minar" width="278" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wasir kasab · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Qutb_Minar_2022.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…and artistic traditions fused with Indian forms to produce a distinctive Indo-Islamic synthesis, visible in architecture from the <b>Qutb Minar complex</b> and Alauddin's Alai Darwaza to the Tughlaq tombs, in the poetry of Amir Khusrau, and in the emergence of the Hindavi vernacular that would later give rise…</blockquote><a href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Ghiyath al-Din rebuilt the Friday Mosque of Herat, and Aybak began the <b>Qutb Minar</b> at Delhi, which Iltutmish completed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
